# Full-Flow Project Documentation & Architecture Blueprint

## Executive Summary
This document provides a **complete, step-by-step chronological account** of the **NavIC-Assisted Intelligent Dead Reckoning System** created for vehicular localization in GNSS-denied environments. It covers every phase of development—from initial project setup, mathematical modeling, sensor integration, AI model training, fusion engine linking, unit testing, to local server deployment.

---

## 1. Project Objectives & Sensors Overview

### Core Problem
Vehicular navigation systems rely heavily on satellite positioning (GNSS/NavIC/GPS). However, in **tunnels, dense urban canyons, and deep forest canopies**, GNSS signals suffer total outages or severe multi-path interference. 

### Objective
Build a robust, lightweight, and mathematically defensible **Dead Reckoning (DR)** navigation engine using smartphone sensors to maintain accurate vehicle positioning during prolonged GNSS outages without position divergence.

### Sensors Utilized

| Sensor | Sampling Rate | Measurement | Role in Pipeline |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **3-Axis Accelerometer** | 100 Hz | $a_x, a_y, a_z$ ($\text{m/s}^2$) | Kinematic velocity & position integration; IMU feature extraction for AI speed inference. |
| **3-Axis Gyroscope** | 100 Hz | $\omega_x, \omega_y, \omega_z$ ($\text{rad/s}$) | 3D attitude & orientation propagation via quaternion kinematics ($\mathbf{q}$). |
| **3-Axis Magnetometer** | 10–50 Hz | $B_x, B_y, B_z$ ($\mu\text{T}$) | Absolute heading calibration & magnetic declination reference. |
| **NavIC L5 / GPS Receiver** | 1–10 Hz | Latitude, Longitude, Altitude, GNSS Speed, CEP Accuracy | High-precision position/velocity measurement updates during healthy GNSS availability. |

